On Mon, 2011-08-22 at 18:15 +0200, Håvard Rue wrote:
> On Mon, 2011-08-22 at 08:36 -0700, Márcio Laurini wrote:
> > My question is about the marginal likelihood in general state space
> > formulations.
> > For example,  using the example in Ruiz-Cardenas R., Krainski E.T. and
> > Rue H. (2010)  for  the estimation of  a local level model using the
> > general state space  formulation and the same estimation using the
> > latent  RW model,  we obtain very different values for the  marginal
> > likelihood, which I believe is due to the construction  of the
> > artificial state vectors in the  state space formulation.
> > It’s possible to obtain the correct value of the marginal likelihood
> > ignoring these artificial state vectors?
> >
> > Márcio
> >
> >
> > #code
> >
> >
> > # Simulating observations and states from a first order DLM
> > # (based on code by Hedibert Lopes
> > <http://faculty.chicagobooth.edu/hedibert.lopes/>
> > # ---------------------------------------------------------
> > set.seed(123456)
> >
> > W = 0.5
> > V = 1.0
> > n = 100
> > x0 = 0.00
> > w  = rnorm(n,0,sqrt(W))
> > v  = rnorm(n,0,sqrt(V))
> > x  = y = rep(0,n)
> > x[1] = x0   + w[1]
> > y[1] = x[1] + v[1]
> > for (t in 2:n){
> >   x[t] = x[t-1] + w[t]
> >   y[t] = x[t]   + v[t]
> > }
> >
> > m = n-1
> >
> > # Plotting the simulated series
> > # -----------------------------
> > plot(y,type="l",xlab="time",ylab="")
> > lines(x,col=2)
> > legend("topright",legend=c("y","x"), col=c("black",
> > "red"),lty=c(1,1),bty="n")
> > title(paste("V=",V," ; W=",W,sep=""))
> >
> >
> > # building the augmented model
> > # ----------------------------
> > Y <- matrix(NA, n+m, 2)
> > Y[1:n,     1] <- y              # actual observations
> > Y[1:m + n, 2] <- 0              # faked observations
>
> yes, you'll get a contribution for the
>
> Y[1:m + n, 2] <- 0              # faked observations
>
> part of the data as well. which are just 'fake' data therefore it should
> not be there.
>
> see if the `error' in the mlik  is about
>
> n* (  1/2*log(2*pi)  + 1/2 * log( exp(10) ) )

hmm, I forgot the sqr-term, so it should be

n* (  1/2*log(2*pi)  + 1/2 * log( exp(10) )  -1/2 )

